Q: FlowRow make elements in row stretch to available width A: <p>From Compose BOM 2026.04.01 onwards there is now a new (still experimental) <strong><a href="https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/compose/layouts/adaptive/flexbox" rel="nofollow noreferrer">FlexBox</a></strong> composable. It combines the FlowRow and FlowColumn functionality and adds additional configuration options:</p>
<pre><code>FlexBox(config = {
    wrap(FlexWrap.Wrap)
    gap(10.dp)
}) {
    for (name in nameOptions) {
        Name(
            name = name,
            modifier = Modifier.flex {
                grow(1f)
            },
        )
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>I extracted the Text into its own composable to keep the remaining code more clean:</p>
<pre><code>@Composable
fun Name(name: String, modifier: Modifier = Modifier) {
    Text(
        text = name,
        softWrap = false,
        textAlign = TextAlign.Center,
        modifier = modifier
            .border(
                width = 1.dp,
                color = Color.Black,
                shape = RoundedCornerShape(15.dp),
            )
            .padding(10.dp),
    )
}
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><p>By default the FlexBox doesn't wrap its children. That's why <code>wrap(FlexWrap.Wrap)</code> is needed.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>gap(10.dp)</code> specifies that all elements should be spaced by 10.dp. That applies to horizontal and vertical spacing alike. Use <code>rowGap</code> and <code>columnGap</code> to apply separate spacings.</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>grow(1f)</code> tells an element that it should grow to consume the remaining horizontal space. This is applied to <em>all</em> elements here, so they share the remaining space in each row, only consuming an <em>equal</em> fraction each. This also applies to the last element which happens to be the only one in its row, so it will grow to occupy it in its entirety:</p>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/bZKamSCU.png" alt="" /></p>
<p>I don't think there is a way to determine if an element is placed in the <em>last</em> row so that you can omit <code>grow(1f)</code> for those elements. That would, however, be necessary to achieve exactly what you requested. For that you still need to create your own layout, as the other answer already demonstrates.</p>
</li>
</ul>

Q: How to fix my LINQ expressions with Distinct() in it? A: <p><code>ForEach</code> is a function of a <code>List&lt;T&gt;</code>, not of a more general <code>IEnumerable&lt;T&gt;</code> (which is what <code>Distinct</code> gives you).</p>
<p>While it is possible to use <code>ToList</code>, as you have done elsewhere, that is inefficient. The more correct thing is to just iterate with a proper <code>foreach</code> loop.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>foreach (var theThing in számlák.OrderBy(x =&gt; x.KöltségTípus).Select(x =&gt; x.KöltségTípus).Distinct())
{
    sw.WriteLine(theThing);
}
</code></pre>
